g as- ... We prove the rationality phenomenon in a few\nConnectivity patterns in loop percolation I:\nthe rationality phenomenon and constant term\nidentities\nDan Romik\nSeptember 20, 2013\nAbstract\nLoop percolation, also known as the dense Op1q loop model, is\na variant of critical bond percolation in the square lattice Z2 whose\ngraph structure consists of a disjoint union of cycles. We study its\nconnectivity pattern, which is a random noncrossing matching as-\nsociated with a loop percolation conﬁguration.\nThese connectivity\npatterns exhibit a striking rationality property whereby probabilities\nof naturally-occurring events are dyadic rational numbers or rational\nfunctions of a size parameter n, but the reasons for this are not com-\npletely understood. We prove the rationality phenomenon in a few\ncases and prove an explicit formula expressing the probabilities in the\n“cylindrical geometry” as coeﬃcients in certain multivariate polyno-\nmials.\nThis reduces the rationality problem in the general case to\nthat of proving a family of conjectural constant term identities gener-\nalizing an identity due to Di Francesco and Zinn-Justin. Our results\nmake use of, and extend, algebraic techniques related to the quantum\nKnizhnik-Zamolodchikov equation.\nKey words: loop percolation, Op1q loop model, XXZ spin chain, noncrossing matching,\nconnectivity pattern, constant term identity, quantum Knizhnik-Zamolodchikov equation,\nwheel polynomials\n2010 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation: 60K35, 82B20, 82B23.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConnectivity patterns in loop percolation I: the rationality phenomenon and constant term identities (https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.6341)\nciteturn12academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.6 years ago; Title: Connectivity patterns in loop percolation I: the rationality phenomenon and constant term identities ... We study its connectivity pattern, which is a random noncrossing matching associated with a loop percolation configuration.\nTitle: Connectivity patterns in loop percolation I: the rationality phenomenon and constant term identities\nAuthors: Dan Romik\nDate: Mon Mar 25 22:44:17 2013\n\nLoop percolation, also known as the dense O(1) loop model, is a variant of critical bond percolation in the square lattice Z^2 whose graph structure consists of a disjoint union of cycles. We study its connectivity pattern, which is a random noncrossing matching associated with a loop percolation configuration. These connectivity patterns exhibit a striking rationality property whereby probabilities of naturally-occurring events are dyadic rational numbers or rational functions of a size parameter n, but the reasons for this are not completely understood. We prove the rationality phenomenon in a few cases and prove an explicit formula expressing the probabilities in the \"cylindrical geometry\" as coefficients in certain multivariate polynomials. This reduces the rationality problem in the general case to that of proving a family of conjectural constant term identities generalizing an identity due to Di Francesco and Zinn-Justin. Our results make use of, and extend, algebraic techniques related to the quantum Knizhnik-Zamolodchikov equation.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharge on tableaux and the poset of k-shapes (https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.2438)\nciteturn12academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.5 years ago; Title: Charge on tableaux and the poset of k-shapes ... The main ingredient in this construction was a bijection, which we call the weak bijection, that associates to a k-tableau a pair made out of a (k-1)-tableau and a path in the poset of k-shapes.\nTitle: Charge on tableaux and the poset of k-shapes\nAuthors: Luc Lapointe, Maria Elena Pinto\nDate: Fri May 10 20:49:51 2013\n\nA poset on a certain class of partitions known as k-shapes was recently introduced to provide a combinatorial rule f